Task: Decide whether the priors need updating based on the reflection. If the existing priors
already cover this run's lessons adequately, do not update — set should_update to false and
explain why in diff_summary. Otherwise, update the priors: emphasize what the triage scan
missed or should have covered. Preserve guidance that was not implicated unless the reflection
shows it is wrong or harmful.

Document shape (required):
- Use one markdown `##` heading per focus area. The heading text is a short title you choose
  for that area (from the incident — not from any fixed taxonomy).
- Under each `##`, write the body for that area only: a short paragraph and/or `-` bullets.
- Each bullet: one sentence; keep bodies compact so the full file stays readable.

Mutual exclusivity (required):
- **No overlap between `##` sections.** If the same kubectl observation, log line, or pattern
  could plausibly belong under two headings, merge those sections into one `##` or narrow scopes
  until each observation fits at most one section.
- Prefer fewer disjoint sections over many redundant ones.

When to add a section or expand a body:
- The reflection shows triage missed something that mattered for the outcome.

When to remove, merge, or narrow:
- A section produces benign noise or duplicates another — merge or delete.
- Two sections overlap — merge per the rule above.

Rules:
- Maximum 12 `##` sections total.
- All wording must be generic (no app-specific names, hostnames, or literal config values).
- Do not restate the agent's built-in triage methodology; only add learned gaps from this run.
- In updated_content, output markdown only: `##` sections and bodies, no preamble or commentary before the first `##`.
- In diff_summary, write a numbered list where each item explains one edit decision and why
  (e.g. "1. Added section 'Pod scheduling' — triage missed pending pods that indicated resource
  pressure\n2. Merged 'Log patterns' into 'Error signals' — overlapping scope"). If not
  updating, explain why the existing priors are sufficient.

